lmn-gpo (1.13.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * New OPT-IN pack 19-home-h: maps the home drive H: a second time via Group Policy
    Preferences (Drive Maps), in addition to the homeDrive/homeDirectory attributes that
    linuxmuster already sets. Off unless home_drive_enabled is set; the setup wizard asks.
    It does NOT make H: appear sooner - Winlogon still connects it first during session
    setup. It closes the case where Winlogon fails because the network is not up yet
    (typical on 802.1X notebooks) and H: silently stays missing: the Drive Maps CSE runs
    later in the same logon, and unlike Winlogon it is re-invoked when the machine regains
    connectivity to a domain controller ([MS-GPOL] 3.2.7.1).
    The path is NOT hardcoded per school or role. An LDAP query targeting item reads the
    user's own homeDirectory into a variable that the same item's path then expands, so a
    single entry covers every school, role and class - which is required, because student
    homes carry the class in the path and there is no preference variable for a class.
  * Verified on a real Windows 11 24H2 client, not inferred from documentation: that a
    variable produced by FilterLdap really expands in the path of the SAME item was the
    one load-bearing unknown. Proven with a second item on a letter Winlogon never maps
    (H: alone proves nothing - Winlogon maps it identically from the AD attribute). Both
    a teacher (\\server\<school>\teachers\<user>) and a student with a class in the
    path (\\server\<school>\students\<class>\<user>) mapped correctly. RSoP reported
    ErrorCount=0 and ProcessingAppMode=ForegroundSync, and H: existed exactly once - no
    collision with Winlogon's own mapping.
  * Fail-closed, also observed rather than assumed: on an account whose home directory did
    not exist on disk, neither Winlogon nor the preference mapped anything, and the other
    drives were unaffected thanks to bypassErrors="1". No home directory, no unreachable
    share, no mapping - never a wrong one.
  * action="U" (Update) deliberately, not Replace: on a letter that already holds a working
    mapping it is a no-op, and it can never tear down a working H: that it then fails to
    re-create. useLetter="0" is never emitted and CI asserts it - per KB3091116 it means
    "delete all, starting at" and would wipe every mapping from that letter through Z:.
  * Drive Maps is USER policy: the CSE is registered on gPCUserExtensionNames and the USER
    half of versionNumber is bumped (Groups.xml is the machine counterpart). CI asserts the
    pack resolves to a user GPO, since a machine GPO here would silently do nothing.
  * Known limitation, stated rather than hidden: a client-side extension reapplies only when
    the GPO changed, so the self-healing retry after the network returns needs the "process
    even if the GPOs have not changed" option for Drive Maps. That registry value is NOT yet
    shipped - it is unverified, and shipping an unverified reprocess-every-refresh setting
    would be a side effect of its own. Within a single logon the safety net works today.
  * sophomorix' own GPO is never touched; removing the pack removes the mapping and leaves
    exactly the behaviour linuxmuster has without it.
  * Version 1.12.0 is deliberately skipped. It was published and then withdrawn on request,
    and reusing the number for different content would be worse than leaving a gap.

